Search products by name (text) AND category (keyword) AND price range, ranked by relevance:

POST /products/_search
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "must": [
        { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } }
      ],
      "filter": [
        { "term": { "category": "footwear" } },
        { "range": { "price": { "gte": 50, "lte": 200 } } },
        { "term": { "in_stock": true } }
      ]
    }
  }
}

Note: name uses match (text field, analyzed). category and in_stock use term (keyword/boolean, exact). price uses range. All non-scoring conditions are in filter context for caching.

Beyond match, term, and bool, three advanced query types solve specific real-world problems that interview questions probe.

**function_score — custom relevance with boosting functions**. The default BM25 score ranks by text relevance, but production search often needs to combine relevance with business signals: recency (newer documents rank higher), popularity (more views = higher rank), distance (nearer locations rank higher), or A/B test boosts. The `function_score` query wraps a base query and modifies its score with one or more functions:

```json
POST /products/_search
{
  "query": {
    "function_score": {
      "query": { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } },
      "functions": [
        {
          "gauss": {
            "created_at": {
              "origin": "now",
              "scale": "30d",
              "decay": 0.5
            }
          },
          "weight": 1.5
        },
        {
          "field_value_factor": {
            "field": "popularity_score",
            "modifier": "log1p",
            "missing": 1
          }
        },
        {
          "filter": { "term": { "in_stock": true } },
          "weight": 2.0
        }
      ],
      "score_mode": "sum",
      "boost_mode": "multiply"
    }
  }
}
```

This query matches by name, then boosts recent products (Gaussian decay over 30 days), boosts popular products (log scale to dampen extreme outliers), and doubles the score for in-stock items. The `score_mode` controls how function scores combine (sum, multiply, max, avg); `boost_mode` controls how the function score combines with the query score.

**dis_max (disjunction max) — best-of-several-matches scoring**. When searching across multiple fields, the standard `bool.should` adds scores from each match, which can produce strange results when one document matches many fields weakly while another matches one field strongly. `dis_max` instead takes the maximum of the per-clause scores plus a tiebreaker fraction of the others:

```json
{
  "query": {
    "dis_max": {
      "queries": [
        { "match": { "title": "spring boot" } },
        { "match": { "description": "spring boot" } },
        { "match": { "tags": "spring boot" } }
      ],
      "tie_breaker": 0.3
    }
  }
}
```

A document matching only `title` strongly wins over a document matching all three fields weakly. The `tie_breaker: 0.3` adds 30% of each non-max clause's score, breaking pure ties between equally strong matches. `multi_match` with `type: best_fields` is syntactic sugar for `dis_max`.

**Span queries — position-aware matching**. For technical/legal/medical search where word order and proximity carry meaning, span queries enable position-sensitive matching beyond `match_phrase`. `span_near` matches when terms appear within N positions of each other; `span_first` matches when terms appear within the first N positions of the field; `span_or` and `span_not` compose spans with logical operators:

```json
{
  "query": {
    "span_near": {
      "clauses": [
        { "span_term": { "body": "spring" } },
        { "span_term": { "body": "boot" } },
        { "span_term": { "body": "3.2" } }
      ],
      "slop": 5,
      "in_order": true
    }
  }
}
```

This matches "Spring Boot 3.2", "Spring Boot version 3.2", and "Spring with Boot at 3.2" but not "3.2 announced for Spring Boot". Span queries are slower than match queries (must validate positions, can't use simple posting list intersection) but enable precise control when needed.

**explain mode for debugging**. Add `"explain": true` to any query to see exactly how each document's score was computed — which clauses contributed, what the BM25 components were, how function_score modifications applied. This is the fastest way to debug "why is document X ranked above document Y?" questions in production. The output is verbose but every score in the result becomes traceable to specific term frequencies, IDF values, and field length normalizations.

