Real Elasticsearch integrations need bulk indexing for throughput, retry policies for transient failures, and zero-downtime index migrations. Spring Data Elasticsearch supports all three but requires explicit configuration.

**Bulk indexing for high throughput**. Single-document `save()` calls are an anti-pattern for ingestion: each call is a separate HTTP request with its own refresh cycle. Bulk operations batch hundreds or thousands of documents into a single request, increasing throughput by 10-100x:

```java
@Service
public class ProductIndexer {
    private final ElasticsearchOperations ops;

public void bulkIndex(List<ProductDocument> products) {
        List<IndexQuery> queries = products.stream()
            .map(p -> new IndexQueryBuilder()
                .withId(p.getId())
                .withObject(p)
                .build())
            .toList();
        ops.bulkIndex(queries, IndexCoordinates.of("products"));
    }
}
```

For very large initial loads, disable refresh and replicas, bulk-index, then re-enable: set `index.refresh_interval=-1` and `number_of_replicas=0`, run the bulk load, then restore both. This eliminates per-batch refresh overhead and replication writes during the load. A 100M-document load that takes 8 hours with default settings can complete in 90 minutes with this tuning.

**Resilience4j retries for transient failures**. Elasticsearch can return 429 (too many requests), 503 (service unavailable), or connection timeouts during shard rebalancing or temporary load spikes. Wrap calls with retry logic:

```java
@Retryable(
    retryFor = { ElasticsearchException.class, IOException.class },
    maxAttempts = 4,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 200, multiplier = 2, maxDelay = 5000)
)
public SearchHits<ProductDocument> searchWithRetry(Query query) {
    return ops.search(query, ProductDocument.class);
}

@Recover
public SearchHits<ProductDocument> recoverFromES(
        ElasticsearchException ex, Query query) {
    log.error("ES query failed after retries, falling back to DB", ex);
    return SearchHits.empty();
}
```

Don't retry on 4xx client errors — those indicate bad queries that won't succeed on retry. Only retry on transient 5xx errors and IO exceptions.

**Zero-downtime index migration via aliases**. When a document mapping changes (new field type, new analyzer, new sub-field), the only safe option is to reindex into a new index. Aliases enable this without downtime:

```java
@Service
public class IndexMigrationService {
    private final ElasticsearchOperations ops;
    private final IndicesClient indicesClient;

public void migrateProducts() {
        String oldIndex = "products_v1";
        String newIndex = "products_v2";
        String alias = "products";

ops.indexOps(IndexCoordinates.of(newIndex)).create(newSettings(), newMapping());

ops.reindex(Reindex.builder(
            IndexCoordinates.of(oldIndex),
            IndexCoordinates.of(newIndex)
        ).build());

IndicesAliasesRequest aliasUpdate = new IndicesAliasesRequest()
            .addAliasAction(IndicesAliasesRequest.AliasActions.add()
                .index(newIndex).alias(alias))
            .addAliasAction(IndicesAliasesRequest.AliasActions.remove()
                .index(oldIndex).alias(alias));
        indicesClient.updateAliases(aliasUpdate);

ops.indexOps(IndexCoordinates.of(oldIndex)).delete();
    }
}
```

Application code always reads and writes through the alias, never the physical index name.
- The reindex copies all documents (use `slices: auto` for parallelism on large indices), the alias swap is atomic from the cluster's perspective, and the application never sees downtime.
- For ongoing writes during the migration, also enable dual-write to both old and new indices, then switch reads via alias once the reindex completes — this handles the case where new documents arrive during the multi-hour reindex of a large index.

