// Inspect shard placement
GET /_cat/shards/orders?v
// index  shard prirep state   node
// orders 0     p      STARTED node-1     ← primary 0 on node-1
// orders 0     r      STARTED node-2     ← replica of 0 on node-2
// orders 0     r      STARTED node-3
// orders 1     p      STARTED node-2
// orders 1     r      STARTED node-1
// orders 1     r      STARTED node-3
// ...
```

- **Number of primary shards** is fixed at index creation — plan for ~20-40 GB per shard; splitting later means reindexing. A 600 GB index → ~20 primaries.
- **Replicas** multiply query throughput (each replica can serve reads) and provide HA — a 3-node cluster with `number_of_replicas: 1` survives one node failure without data loss.
- **Rule of thumb**: `total_shards = primaries × (1 + replicas)`. This index uses 3 × 3 = 9 shards — exactly 3 per node.
- A **document's shard** is `hash(_routing) % number_of_primary_shards`; by default `_routing = _id`. Custom routing lets you co-locate a tenant's documents on one shard to reduce query fan-out.
- **Master-eligible** nodes coordinate cluster state; **data** nodes hold shards; **ingest** nodes run pipelines. In small clusters all roles overlap; at scale, separate them.

