```java
// ---------- Elasticsearch (separate store, CDC-synced) ----------
@RestController
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ProductSearchController {
    private final ElasticsearchClient es;

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public SearchResult search(@RequestParam String q,
                               @RequestParam(required = false) Double maxPrice) throws IOException {

        SearchResponse<Product> resp = es.search(s -> s
            .index("products")
            .query(qb -> qb.bool(b -> b
                .must(m -> m.multiMatch(mm -> mm
                    .query(q)
                    .fields("name^2", "description")))
                .filter(f -> f.range(r -> r.field("price").lte(JsonData.of(maxPrice))))))
            .aggregations("by_category",
                a -> a.terms(t -> t.field("category.keyword").size(10)))
            .highlight(h -> h.fields("description",
                HighlightField.of(hf -> hf.numberOfFragments(1)))),
            Product.class);

        return SearchResult.from(resp);
    }
}
```

| Aspect | Atlas Search | Elasticsearch |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Separate store | no — lives inside MongoDB | yes — sync required |
| Operational overhead | low — one cluster | medium — cluster + pipeline |
| Query language | `$search` stage in aggregation | DSL or SQL |
| Faceting / aggregations | good | best-in-class |
| Geo, vector, kNN | supported | supported (vector DBs emerging) |
| Cost model | bundled with Atlas tier | separate infra / managed service |
| Best fit | MongoDB-centric apps, moderate search needs | large-scale search, analytics, multi-source |

- **Atlas Search** wins on simplicity: no CDC, no second store, queries run in the same transaction boundary as reads. Good for catalogs up to ~10M docs and straightforward relevance.
- **Elasticsearch** wins on ceiling: billions of documents, complex scoring and relevance tuning, cross-index joins via parent/child, heavy analytics with buckets, vector search. Pay for it with operational complexity.
- Consistency: Atlas Search indexes near-real-time from the oplog (sub-second); Elasticsearch lag depends on your CDC pipeline (usually 1-5 seconds).
- Rule of thumb: start with **Atlas Search** if you're on MongoDB and your search needs are straightforward; move to **Elasticsearch** when relevance tuning, analytics, or multi-source indexing become primary requirements.
